A||||7||Roy||Overheating!!
Z||000000||Roy||05-22-99||01:28 AM||||I need some help guy's I have no idea whats causing the car to overheat. After running it for about a hour the car climbs to 260, it died once on me on the high way, check it the water level is fine. There isn't a blown head seal, What are some other things I could check?
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||05-22-99||01:38 AM||||Do you hear <i>gurgling sounds</i> when the engine is getting hot? Or strange ticks (both soft as very loud ticks) coming from under your car? If so, then there's air in your water system. <p>Also, check your thermostat (with a cold engine!). See if it is still there in the first place. A lot of people remove it. If your thermostat is still there and you are sure it is working properly, check your oil level and the color of your oil. If it is pitch black, you need to replace it <b>soon</b>! Could be that it is filled with the wrong oil too.<p>And ofcourse, check if the radiator fan comes on. Good luck!
Z||000002||theogre||05-22-99||11:07 AM||||Assuming the thermostat and water pump are good..... and no air is in the system, no plumbing blockages, make sure there is no crud blocking the front of the radiator. <p>find the fan temp switch, and jump the terminal(s) in the connector to ground. the fan should come on imediatly. (if your switch has 2 terminals... you have the 2 speed fan. test both speeds.)<p>there is no easy way to test it w/o removing it. Even if the switch is good.... you'll likely boil over before it activates.<p>The turnon point for the single speed fan is 235F/113C. The 2 speed activates low at 221F/105C and hi at 246F/119C.<p>By the time either fan system activates full speed, you are running way hot. Even the low speed may no be coming on soon enough in many settings.
Z||000003||Carrolles||05-24-99||10:00 AM||||While travelling at highway speed the fan is not a factor in cooling the engine.  You get much more cooling just from airflow around the car than the fan could provide.  If the thermostat is stuck closed it doesn't matter how fast you drive or how powerful a fan you have, your coolant isn't making the trip through the radiator.<p>I'd start with the basics.  Purge tha air out of the system per the instructions in the manual.  <br>Check your water pump belt.  Check to see that the thermostat is not stuck closed.  Look at the coolant to see if it is full of crud which could be blocking flow in the radiator.<br>See if you have a coolant pipe collapsed under the car.    There are two metal pipes that run from the engine bay to the front compartment. There is one on each side of the car.  Each has a drain plug that can be removed for draining the line or checking to see if the line is cruded up.  If the car was ever high centered on a rock or a curb one of these pipes could have been crushed.<br>Also check the water pump.  With the engine cold remove the cap at the thermostat housing to see if coolant is flowing when the engine is running.<p>Look at your hoses to see if they are swollen at the clamp area.  When these hoses get old they will sometimes delaminate at the hose clamp.  The inside layer of rubber will peal away and form a sort of internal check valve.  If the hoses are soft and swollen take them loose and see if this has happened.<p>Good luck<br>Carroll<p><br>
Z||000004||87GT||05-24-99||05:00 PM||||When I bought my Fiero, the owner was telling me about a overheating problem he experienced.  He took it to his best friend which was mechanic and they could not find the problem. THey looked all over for it and never could find it.  They finally figured where the problem was, it was a sensor in the front of the car.  If you pop the front lid and look at the front left(if your sitting inside)all the way up front, there is a small sensor sitting there.  The original owner told me that they replaced it and it cured the problem.  He also mentioned that that sensor was not documented in any Pontiac Fiero repair manuals.  Strange isnt it?  I hope I got the story right. 8-)  Good luck!
Z||000005||Patrick||05-24-99||08:24 PM||||<br>I think you are referring to the fan <b>relay</b>. Quite a story though. <IMG SRC="http://forums.gagames.com/forums/smile.gif"> <p>
Z||000006||87GT||05-24-99||09:42 PM||||I asked the owner that question about it being a fan relay and he said it wasnt. Maybe he has no idea what he is talking about.  But, I tried. :-)
Z||000007||theogre||05-24-99||10:08 PM||||Carrolle,<p>You might want to try locking the fan on some time and testing it. It can, and does, make a significant differance even on a moving car. The fan at full speed draws a couple thousand CFM thru the radiator. The boost this gives even a moving Fiero is far more than you would think. Don't count on the temp gauge to tell you what's really going on. it's not acurate or fast enough. you have to use a digital thermometer, or monitor the ECM sensor.<p>I've tested it repeatedly now on a known good plumbing system. Operating temp with the fan on stayed in a 10-15 degree F window mostly from about 208-215F which is the norm for every other cast iron motor I've tested. Without the fan, the in motion temp stayed at 220F or more, and stoped for a red light temp quickly jumped 10-15 more. In no case did the fan switch activate.<p>I've tested cooling on a bunch of cast iron over the years. I've never seen the kind of thermal swinging Fiero has, not even in HO v8 engines. The whole purpose of the cooling system is to keep the engine at a constant temp at or close to the thermostat value. The ECM programming in fact depends on the engine being in a fairly narrow temperature window. Fiero fails miserably to do this without the fan.<p>One of these days I want to take and put a clear cover on the front and smoke it to see just how the air flows. Judging by the thermal behavior of the system, I expect there is allot of dead air backing up behind the radiator.<p>I also expect to see allot of air going in other directions besides thru the radiator and AC condenser since the ram tunnel is open to either side which will let air move thru the fenders. Add to that the resriction of an AC condenser in front of the radiator and very little air gets to the radiator to start with. Then what air does get thru piles up behind waiting to get out.<p>If the fan does nothing but stir the air space at highway speed, it's still an improvement. The fact that the temp now doesn't jump 15+ degrees at every red light is also a good thing.
